Published  <time datetime="2017-09-19T00:00:00.000Z"> 19 September 2017 </time> </span> </div> <h1 class="mb-8 lg:mb-10 text-balance">Acute Injury First Aid: What to Do and What to Avoid</h1> <div class="pb-6 border-b border-border flex items-start gap-4"> <img src="/_astro/charbel-daher.BBIQAnvc_Z89N2D.webp" srcset="/_astro/charbel-daher.BBIQAnvc_KjPhK.webp 640w, /_astro/charbel-daher.BBIQAnvc_ZCb6FV.webp 750w, /_astro/charbel-daher.BBIQAnvc_1kHi7j.webp 828w, /_astro/charbel-daher.BBIQAnvc_1wEAPU.webp 1080w, /_astro/charbel-daher.BBIQAnvc_1DsyrD.webp 1280w, /_astro/charbel-daher.BBIQAnvc_Z89N2D.webp 1600w" alt="Charbel Daher, Chiropractor" loading="lazy" decoding="async" sizes="(min-width: 1600px) 1600px, 100vw" data-astro-image="constrained" data-astro-image-fit="cover" data-astro-image-pos="center" width="1600" height="1067" class="object-cover w-12 h-12 rounded-pill bg-surface-alt shrink-0"> <div class="flex-1 min-w-0" data-article-byline> <p class="text-meta leading-snug mb-0.5"> <a href="/team/charbel-daher/" class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-sage transition-colors"> Charbel Daher </a> <span class="text-muted mx-1.5" aria-hidden="true">
·
</span> <span class="text-muted" data-byline-metadata> Chiropractor<span class="mx-1.5" aria-hidden="true">
·
</span>  5 min read
</span> </p> <p class="text-fine text-muted">
Reviewed  <time datetime="2025-09-19T00:00:00.000Z"> 19 September 2025 </time> </p> </div> <div class="flex items-center gap-1 shrink-0" data-print-hide> <button type="button" data-print-article aria-label="Print this article" title="Print" class="p-2 rounded-md text-muted hover:text-primary hover:bg-surface-alt transition-colors"> <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" class="w-4 h-4" data-icon="lucide:printer">   <symbol id="ai:lucide:printer"><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 18H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2v-5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h16a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v5a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-2M6 9V3a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h10a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v6"/><rect width="12" height="8" x="6" y="14" rx="1"/></g></symbol><use href="#ai:lucide:printer"></use>  </svg> </button>  </div> </div>  </div>  </div> </header>   <script type="module" src="/_astro/TableOfContents.astro_astro_type_script_index_0_lang.DQaRKSb9.js"></script><section data-component="Section" data-rhythm="none" data-tone="none" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6="true" class="pb-(--spacing-section-y-tight)">  <div class="mx-auto px-4 max-w-(--max-w-prose) lg:max-w-(--layout-post-outer)">  <div class="@container" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6> <div class="@[60rem]:grid @[60rem]:grid-cols-[minmax(0,var(--layout-post-body))_var(--layout-post-sidebar)] @[60rem]:gap-(--layout-post-gap)" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6> <div class="prose prose--hh max-w-none" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6>  <aside class="not-prose my-8 lg:mt-0 rounded-lg bg-surface-tinted border border-border-tinted p-6 lg:p-7" data-speakable="key-takeaways"><p class="text-data-label uppercase text-primary font-semibold mb-4 pb-3 border-b border-border-tinted">Key takeaways</p><ul class="space-y-3 list-none p-0 m-0"><li class="flex gap-3 items-start"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="text-body leading-snug">The first 48-72 hours after a soft-tissue injury shape how well it settles. Early decisions matter.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 items-start"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="text-body leading-snug">Current guidance favours protection, unloading, and gentle movement over strict rest and prolonged icing.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 items-start"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="text-body leading-snug">Four things actively slow healing: heat, alcohol, running and exercise, and deep massage directly over the injury site.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 items-start"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="text-body leading-snug">Swelling and pain are part of the body&#39;s normal repair response. The goal is to support that process, not suppress it entirely.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3 items-start"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="text-body leading-snug">Book an assessment if pain is severe, swelling is significant, or the injury isn&#39;t improving after a few days.</span></li></ul></aside>
<p>When you hurt yourself (a rolled ankle, a pulled muscle, a knocked-up knee), the choices you make in the first 48-72 hours can either support the body’s repair process or slow it down. This guide covers current first-aid principles for soft-tissue injuries and the four things most likely to make things worse.</p>
<h2 id="why-does-the-early-phase-matter">Why does the early phase matter?</h2>
<p>When soft tissue is injured, the body responds immediately with <strong>inflammation</strong>: blood flow increases to the area, white blood cells flood in, and the familiar signs follow: redness, swelling, heat, and pain. This is a normal, necessary repair process, not simply something to be suppressed.</p>
<p>The goal of early first aid is to protect the injured tissue from further harm and create conditions where the body can do its job. Doing too little can leave the injury vulnerable; doing the wrong things can amplify the inflammatory response and delay healing.</p>
<h2 id="what-youll-need">What you’ll need</h2>
<div class="not-prose space-y-3.5 my-6"> <p class="leading-relaxed"> <strong class="text-primary">A cold source.</strong>  <span class="text-body">A bag of ice wrapped in a cloth, a cold pack, or a bag of frozen vegetables. Never apply ice directly to skin.</span> </p><p class="leading-relaxed"> <strong class="text-primary">A compression bandage.</strong>  <span class="text-body">An elastic bandage or compression wrap to limit swelling. Snug but not tight enough to cut off circulation.</span> </p><p class="leading-relaxed"> <strong class="text-primary">Something to elevate with.</strong>  <span class="text-body">A pillow, rolled towel, or cushion to raise the injured limb above heart level where possible.</span> </p><p class="leading-relaxed"> <strong class="text-primary">Time and load awareness.</strong>  <span class="text-body">The most important input. Protect the injury from further stress, but avoid complete immobility for longer than necessary.</span> </p> </div>
<h2 id="step-by-step-first-aid-principles-for-a-soft-tissue-injury">Step-by-step: first-aid principles for a soft-tissue injury</h2>
<p>Current guidance from sports medicine research suggests moving away from strict rest towards a more active approach once the initial acute phase settles.<sup><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Protect the area</strong> from further injury. Stop the activity that caused the injury. If weight-bearing is painful, avoid it.</li>
<li><strong>Elevate the limb</strong> above heart level as soon as practical to help limit swelling.</li>
<li><strong>Apply a compression bandage</strong> over the injured area to provide support and reduce swelling. Remove if numbness or tingling develop.</li>
<li><strong>Apply cold</strong> for 15-20 minutes at a time, with at least an hour between applications. Use a cloth between the ice pack and your skin.</li>
<li><strong>Take pain as a guide.</strong> Avoid movements that reproduce sharp pain in the first 24-48 hours. Gentle movement within a pain-free range can help maintain tissue mobility.</li>
<li><strong>Gradually reintroduce load.</strong> Once the acute phase settles (usually after 48-72 hours), gentle, graded movement supports tissue repair better than continued rest. This is where a practitioner can help you understand what the tissue can tolerate.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
<p>The acronym <strong>HARM</strong> captures the four most common things people do after an acute injury that slow the healing process.</p>
<aside class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg border border-border bg-page p-5 lg:p-6"><p class="text-data-label uppercase text-muted font-semibold mb-4">Avoid these in the first 48-72 hours</p><div class="space-y-4"><div class="space-y-1 pb-4 border-b border-border"><p class="font-semibold text-primary">Heat</p><p class="text-meta text-muted leading-relaxed">Heat dilates blood vessels, increasing blood flow to the area. This amplifies swelling and can increase bleeding in injured tissue. Avoid heat packs, hot baths, and saunas.</p></div><div class="space-y-1 pb-4 border-b border-border"><p class="font-semibold text-primary">Alcohol</p><p class="text-meta text-muted leading-relaxed">Alcohol also dilates blood vessels, making swelling and bruising worse. It can interfere with the body&#39;s repair processes and the dehydration it causes can further complicate tissue recovery.</p></div><div class="space-y-1 pb-4 border-b border-border"><p class="font-semibold text-primary">Running and high-intensity exercise</p><p class="text-meta text-muted leading-relaxed">Exercising through an acute injury increases load on damaged tissue, worsens swelling, and raises body temperature. All of these drive more inflammation. Gentle range-of-motion movement is fine; pushing through is not.</p></div><div class="space-y-1"><p class="font-semibold text-primary">Massage directly over the injury site</p><p class="text-meta text-muted leading-relaxed">Hands-on work directly over acutely injured tissue can increase bleeding and inflammation in the first 24-48 hours. If massage is part of your recovery, a practitioner can gauge what the tissue can tolerate at each stage and focus on surrounding areas before working closer to the site.</p></div></div></aside>
<h2 id="when-to-get-help">When to get help</h2>
<p>Most minor soft-tissue injuries (a mild ankle sprain, a small muscle strain) improve noticeably within a few days with sensible early management. A few patterns are worth getting assessed sooner.</p>
<aside class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg bg-surface-tinted border border-border-tinted p-6 lg:p-7"><p class="text-data-label uppercase text-primary font-semibold mb-4 pb-3 border-b border-border-tinted">Book an assessment if</p><div class="space-y-4"><div class="flex gap-3 items-start pb-4 border-b border-divider-tinted"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-0.5">Pain is severe or getting worse</p><p class="text-meta text-muted-strong leading-relaxed">Pain that escalates over the first 24 hours, rather than gradually easing, is worth a clinical look.</p></div></div><div class="flex gap-3 items-start pb-4 border-b border-divider-tinted"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-0.5">The joint feels unstable or is giving way</p><p class="text-meta text-muted-strong leading-relaxed">This pattern can point to significant ligament or structural involvement that needs proper assessment.</p></div></div><div class="flex gap-3 items-start pb-4 border-b border-divider-tinted"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-0.5">Swelling is significant and rapid</p><p class="text-meta text-muted-strong leading-relaxed">Some swelling is normal. A joint that fills quickly and becomes very tense can indicate more serious tissue damage.</p></div></div><div class="flex gap-3 items-start pb-4 border-b border-divider-tinted"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-0.5">There&#39;s numbness, tingling, or loss of movement</p><p class="text-meta text-muted-strong leading-relaxed">These findings suggest nerve or vascular involvement and need prompt assessment.</p></div></div><div class="flex gap-3 items-start"><span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-pill bg-primary shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-0.5">Things aren&#39;t improving after a few days</p><p class="text-meta text-muted-strong leading-relaxed">If the injury isn&#39;t following the expected pattern of gradual improvement, an assessment can identify what&#39;s going on and set a plan.</p></div></div></div></aside>
<p>You can also read about the clinic’s <a href="/services/physiotherapy/">physiotherapy</a> and <a href="/services/chiropractic/">chiropractic</a> services, or call to talk through whether an appointment makes sense for your situation.</p>
<aside class="not-prose mt-5 mb-2 pt-5 border-t border-border"><p class="text-meta text-muted leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-primary">In an emergency:</strong>
if you have severe sudden pain, numbness, or weakness, call 000
          immediately.
</p></aside>
<aside class="not-prose mt-12 rounded-lg bg-surface-alt border border-border px-5 py-4" data-astro-cid-fiaaijqw><p class="text-meta text-body leading-relaxed" data-astro-cid-fiaaijqw>
This article is general information. It's not a substitute for
        professional advice. For advice specific to your situation, speak to a
        qualified practitioner.
</p></aside>
<div class="references-block not-prose mt-10 pt-6 border-t border-border" data-astro-cid-6ritnjv5> <h2 id="references" class="text-data-label uppercase text-muted font-semibold mb-3" data-astro-cid-6ritnjv5> References </h2> <ol class="references-list text-fine text-muted space-y-1.5 list-decimal pl-5" data-astro-cid-6ritnjv5> <li id="ref-1" data-astro-cid-6ritnjv5>Dubois B, Esculier JF. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2019-101253" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Soft-tissue injuries simply need PEACE and LOVE<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>. <em>British Journal of Sports Medicine</em>. 2020;54(2):72-73.</li> </ol> </div> <h2 id="faqs" class>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="not-prose my-6"><div class="space-y-3" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <details name="faq-should-i-ice-an-acute-injury" data-track-faq="should-i-ice-an-acute-injury" class="group border border-border rounded-lg bg-white hover:border-sage open:border-sage transition-colors" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <summary class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-balance text-primary group-hover:text-sage transition-colors" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> Should I ice an acute injury? <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0 text-muted group-hover:text-sage faq-caret" data-icon="lucide:chevron-down">   <use href="#ai:lucide:chevron-down"></use>  </svg> </summary> <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-body" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <p class="leading-relaxed text-pretty" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> Ice can help reduce pain and limit swelling in the first 24-48 hours, but it should not be applied for more than 20 minutes at a time or directly to skin. The goal is pain relief, not eliminating swelling entirely. Some swelling is part of the body&#39;s normal repair process. </p> </div> </details><details name="faq-should-i-ice-an-acute-injury" data-track-faq="when-can-i-return-to-sport-after-an-acute-injury" class="group border border-border rounded-lg bg-white hover:border-sage open:border-sage transition-colors" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <summary class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-balance text-primary group-hover:text-sage transition-colors" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> When can I return to sport after an acute injury? <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0 text-muted group-hover:text-sage faq-caret" data-icon="lucide:chevron-down">   <use href="#ai:lucide:chevron-down"></use>  </svg> </summary> <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-body" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <p class="leading-relaxed text-pretty" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> That depends on the injury, how it&#39;s healing, and what your sport demands. A practitioner can assess how the tissue is responding and guide a graded return. Coming back too soon under load is one of the most common drivers of re-injury. </p> </div> </details><details name="faq-should-i-ice-an-acute-injury" data-track-faq="do-i-need-imaging-after-a-sprain-or-strain" class="group border border-border rounded-lg bg-white hover:border-sage open:border-sage transition-colors" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <summary class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-balance text-primary group-hover:text-sage transition-colors" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> Do I need imaging after a sprain or strain? <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0 text-muted group-hover:text-sage faq-caret" data-icon="lucide:chevron-down">   <use href="#ai:lucide:chevron-down"></use>  </svg> </summary> <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-body" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <p class="leading-relaxed text-pretty" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> Most sprains and strains don&#39;t need imaging. Your practitioner can assess the injury clinically to determine whether a scan adds anything useful. Imaging is more likely if there&#39;s concern about a fracture, significant ligament damage, or the injury isn&#39;t following a typical pattern. </p> </div> </details><details name="faq-should-i-ice-an-acute-injury" data-track-faq="what-s-the-difference-between-rice-and-peace-and-love" class="group border border-border rounded-lg bg-white hover:border-sage open:border-sage transition-colors" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <summary class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-balance text-primary group-hover:text-sage transition-colors" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> What&#39;s the difference between RICE and PEACE and LOVE? <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0 text-muted group-hover:text-sage faq-caret" data-icon="lucide:chevron-down">   <use href="#ai:lucide:chevron-down"></use>  </svg> </summary> <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-body" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> <p class="leading-relaxed text-pretty" data-astro-cid-ledagoj7> RICE (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation) was the standard first-aid approach for decades. PEACE and LOVE, published in the British Journal of Sports Medicine in 2020, updates that thinking. It adds protection and unloading in the early phase, and introduces active recovery: graded movement, exercise, and psychological support once the acute phase settles. The shift moves away from passive rest towards purposeful, guided loading. </p> </div> </details> </div></div> </div> <aside class="hidden @[60rem]:block" aria-label="Article sidebar" data-print-hide data-astro-cid-oessm4o6> <div class="sticky top-24" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6>  <nav class="toc-card" aria-label="Table of contents" data-toc data-astro-cid-yzatfesp><div class="toc-label" data-astro-cid-yzatfesp>In this article</div><ul class="toc-list" data-astro-cid-yzatfesp><li data-astro-cid-yzatfesp><a href="#why-does-the-early-phase-matter" class="toc-link" data-toc-link="why-does-the-early-phase-matter" data-astro-cid-yzatfesp>Why does the early phase matter?</a></li><li data-astro-cid-yzatfesp><a href="#what-youll-need" class="toc-link" data-toc-link="what-youll-need" data-astro-cid-yzatfesp>What you’ll need</a></li><li data-astro-cid-yzatfesp><a href="#step-by-step-first-aid-principles-for-a-soft-tissue-injury" class="toc-link" data-toc-link="step-by-step-first-aid-principles-for-a-soft-tissue-injury" data-astro-cid-yzatfesp>Step-by-step: first-aid principles for a soft-tissue injury</a></li><li data-astro-cid-yzatfesp><a href="#common-mistakes" class="toc-link" data-toc-link="common-mistakes" data-astro-cid-yzatfesp>Common mistakes</a></li><li data-astro-cid-yzatfesp><a href="#when-to-get-help" class="toc-link" data-toc-link="when-to-get-help" data-astro-cid-yzatfesp>When to get help</a></li></ul></nav>  </div> </aside> </div> </div>  </div>  </section>  <script type="module">const y={0:null,1:["09:00","19:00"],2:["09:00","19:00"],3:["09:00","19:00"],4:["09:00","19:00"],5:["09:00","19:00"],6:["07:00","15:00"]},f=["Sunday","Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday","Saturday"],w={Sun:0,Mon:1,Tue:2,Wed:3,Thu:4,Fri:5,Sat:6},h=30;function S(){const o=new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-AU",{timeZone:"Australia/Sydney",hour12:false,weekday:"short",hour:"2-digit",minute:"2-digit"}).formatToParts(new Date),t={};return o.forEach(e=>{e.type!=="literal"&&(t[e.type]=e.value)}),{dow:w[t.weekday],hour:parseInt(t.hour,10),minute:parseInt(t.minute,10)}}function r(o){const[t,e]=o.split(":").map(Number),n=t>=12?"pm":"am",s=(t+11)%12+1;return e===0?`${s}${n}`:`${s}:${String(e).padStart(2,"0")}${n}`}function b(){const{dow:o,hour:t,minute:e}=S(),n=y[o],s=t*60+e;if(n){const[a,l]=n[0].split(":").map(Number),[i,c]=n[1].split(":").map(Number),d=a*60+l,m=i*60+c;if(s>=d&&s<m){const p=m-s;return p<=h?{state:"closing-soon",label:`Closing in ${p} min`}:{state:"open",label:`Open now \xB7 closes ${r(n[1])}`}}if(s<d)return{state:"closed",label:`Closed \xB7 opens today at ${r(n[0])}`}}else return{state:"appointment",label:"Closed today \xB7 by appointment"};for(let a=1;a<=7;a++){const l=(o+a)%7,i=y[l];if(i)return{state:"closed",label:`Closed \xB7 opens ${a===1?"tomorrow":f[l]} at ${r(i[0])}`}}return{state:"closed",label:"Closed"}}function u(){const{state:o,label:t}=b();document.querySelectorAll("[data-status-pill]").forEach(e=>{e.dataset.statusPillState=o;const n=e.querySelector("[data-status-pill-label]");n&&(n.textContent=t)})}window.__statusPillPolling||(window.__statusPillPolling=true,u(),setInterval(u,6e4),window.addEventListener("pageshow",o=>{o.persisted&&u()}));</script><section data-component="Section" data-rhythm="tight" data-tone="alt" data-print-hide="true" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6="true" class="py-(--spacing-section-y-tight) bg-surface-alt">  <div class="mx-auto px-4 max-w-(--max-w-prose) lg:max-w-(--layout-post-outer)">  <div class="@container" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6> <div class="grid gap-8 @[60rem]:grid-cols-[minmax(0,var(--layout-post-body))_var(--layout-post-sidebar)] @[60rem]:gap-(--layout-post-gap)" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6> <div class="@[60rem]:col-start-1 @[60rem]:row-start-1" data-astro-cid-oessm4o6>  <div class="h-full rounded-lg border border-border bg-page p-8 lg:p-10 text-center shadow-card flex flex-col justify-center"> <h2 class="text-subsection font-semibold text-primary mb-3"> Need a hand with something similar? </h2> <p class="text-muted mb-7 max-w-lg mx-auto">Book in for an assessment and we&#39;ll figure out the right care for you.</p> <div class="flex justify-center mb-4"> <a href="/book-appointment/" data-component="BookButton" data-variant="filled" data-size="lg" data-track="bookingCtaClick" data-track-loc="article" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 font-medium rounded-sm px-8 py-4 text-base min-h-14"> Book an Assessment </a> </div> <p class="text-meta text-muted">
